;; TOOL-RFC-005: the prose path-citation lint (DRIFT-DOC-4), in LLMLL.
;;
;; The RFC is docs/design/tool-rfc-005-doc-path-lint.md. The reference this port
;; reproduces is scripts/doc_path_lint.py, and section 8 of the RFC deletes that
;; reference one release after this file lands.
;;
;; THIS GATE DECIDES NOTHING AND MUST KEEP DECIDING NOTHING. It exits 0 whatever
;; it finds, unless `--strict` is passed. The reference's docstring argues the
;; case at length and the short form is: a rationale may legitimately name a
;; path BECAUSE it does not exist, and a gate that can be wrong about correct
;; input teaches people to write worse prose to appease it. Do not "promote"
;; this to fail-closed. That is a language problem, not a scripting one.
;;
;; MODE-CLI-1 IS WHY THIS IS A STATE MACHINE. `:mode cli` emits a pure
;; `print (step args)` with no Command performed and no exit status, so a
;; program that touches the world has to be a stdin-driven step machine in
;; `console` mode. That is the single largest reason a 132-line Python script
;; becomes this.
;;
;; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THREE DESIGN CHOICES THAT WERE MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. All three are RFC
;; section 9 decisions and all three were settled before this file existed.
;;
;; D2. EXISTENCE IS `git ls-files` MEMBERSHIP, NOT A FILESYSTEM PROBE.
;; `os.path.exists` has no LLMLL equivalent. Existence-by-attempt works
;; (`wasi.fs.read` answers RErr on a missing path, measured), but it moves the
;; file's bytes to answer a yes/no question: 77.0 MB across ~1900 calls for this
;; corpus, in a job budgeted under a minute. So the port enumerates the tree
;; ONCE and tests membership. The divergence is real and is recorded rather than
;; hidden: git answers about the INDEX and the reference asks the FILESYSTEM, so
;; an untracked-but-present file is reported here and suppressed there. Measured
;; on a working tree, exactly two paths differ and BOTH are already in the
;; reference's own ALLOW table, carried there because they exist in a working
;; tree and not in a fresh clone. On CI, which checks out fresh, the divergence
;; is zero. Note that the reference already takes its FILE LIST from git and its
;; RESOLUTION from the filesystem; this port makes one mechanism answer both.
;;
;; D3. `regex-match` IS USED, REVERSING THE PRECEDENT IN versiongate.llmll AND
;; shape.llmll. Those modules hand-roll and say so at the site. Their reasons
;; were measured and neither survives here: versiongate cites REGEX-LOWER-1
;; ("does not build"), which SHIPPED at v0.14.96, and shape cites the verified
;; tier, which a string recognizer cannot reach either way. Measured: a
;; `string-contains` recognizer and a `regex-match` recognizer BOTH report
;; `body-fallback` under STRLIT-BODY-1, while a control over ints reports
;; `body-faithful`, so the probe discriminates and the tier cost is zero.
;;
;; TDFA IS NOT PYTHON'S `re`, AND THAT IS THE STANDING COST OF D3.
;; `regex-match` lowers to Text.Regex.TDFA, which is POSIX ERE. Measured: `\b`
;; IS supported, `\d` is NOT (`^N\d+$` does not match "N123"), and inline
;; `(?i)` is NOT (it matches literally and therefore never fires). A comment in
;; this tree asserted the opposite about `\d` for a release. MEASURE ANY NEW
;; PATTERN BEFORE ADDING IT.
;;
;; HIST-PAT BELOW IS SPELLED IN PER-LETTER BRACKET CLASSES ON PURPOSE. That is
;; exact case-insensitivity, which is what the reference's `re.I` means. The
;; cheap approximation, matching `previously|Previously`, passes the live corpus
;; and FAILS on "PREVIOUSLY". Cover cell 9 exists to forbid it.
;;
;; THE SCANNER IS A SPLIT, NOT A CHARACTER FOLD, AND THIS WAS MEASURED.
;; `regex-match` returns bool and captures nothing, so it cannot find WHERE the
;; citations are; that is REGEX-CAPTURE-1. But splitting the body on a backtick
;; recovers exactly the same set, because a citation is backtick-delimited by
;; construction. Every inter-backtick segment is then tested with one
;; `regex-match`. Checked against the reference over the whole live corpus: 955
;; citations across 172 files, 172 files agreeing, ZERO disagreements.
;;
;; TWO SCANNER MODELS WERE TRIED FIRST AND BOTH ARE WRONG. Recorded so nobody
;; re-derives them. (a) Odd-index segments per line ("inside backticks") is
;; wrong because a backtick span may cross a newline, which desyncs the parity
;; for the rest of the line: it missed one citation in
;; docs/design/finding-arg-position-false-safe.md. (b) Odd-index segments over
;; the whole body is far worse, because ONE unbalanced backtick desyncs
;; everything after it: 16 citations found where the reference finds 94, in
;; docs/compiler-team-roadmap.md. Testing EVERY segment has no parity to lose
;; and is what the reference's regex effectively does.
;;
;; SPLIT-EMPTY-1 is why none of this is a per-character fold: `string-split`
;; with an empty separator does not terminate, so there is no character
;; decomposition to fold over, and the other tools scan by folding a LITERAL
;; index list. This port needs no such fold.
;;
;; `string-split` TAKES THE SEPARATOR FIRST (versiongate.llmll:124 measured it).

(module pathlint

  (import wasi.io   (capability stdout :deterministic false))
  (import wasi.fs   (capability read-write "/"))
  (import wasi.proc (capability run :deterministic false))
  (import adjudicate)
  (open adjudicate)

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- record --
  ;; The state carried across steps. Json rather than a wide product type,
  ;; matching docarchive.llmll: a step machine that grows a field should not
  ;; restate every constructor.
  (type Rc (| Rc Json))
  (def-shell f-of [r: Rc] -> Json (match r ((Rc j) j)))
  (def-shell mk   [j: Json] -> Rc (Rc j))

  (def-shell jstr [v: Json k: string] -> string
    (unwrap-or (json-get-string v k) ""))
  (def-shell jint [v: Json k: string] -> int
    (unwrap-or (json-get-int v k) 0))
  (def-shell sets [v: Json k: string s: string] -> Json
    (unwrap-or (json-set v k (json-of-string s)) v))
  (def-shell seti [v: Json k: string n: int] -> Json
    (unwrap-or (json-set v k (json-of-int n)) v))

  (def-shell with-s [r: Rc k: string v: string] -> Rc (mk (sets (f-of r) k v)))
  (def-shell with-i [r: Rc k: string v: int]    -> Rc (mk (seti (f-of r) k v)))

  ;; `tracked` is the whole `git ls-files` output, newline-delimited and with a
  ;; leading and trailing newline so membership can be a delimited substring
  ;; test. Without the delimiters `design/foo.md` would match inside
  ;; `docs/design/foo.md` and every suffix would resolve.
  (def-shell tracked-of [r: Rc] -> string (jstr (f-of r) "tracked"))
  (def-shell queue-of   [r: Rc] -> string (jstr (f-of r) "queue"))
  (def-shell cur-of     [r: Rc] -> string (jstr (f-of r) "cur"))
  (def-shell out-of     [r: Rc] -> string (jstr (f-of r) "out"))
  (def-shell work-of    [r: Rc] -> string (jstr (f-of r) "work"))
  (def-shell cites-of   [r: Rc] -> int    (jint (f-of r) "cites"))
  (def-shell scanned-of [r: Rc] -> int    (jint (f-of r) "scanned"))
  (def-shell found-of   [r: Rc] -> int    (jint (f-of r) "found"))
  (def-shell files-of   [r: Rc] -> int    (jint (f-of r) "files"))
  (def-shell strict-of  [r: Rc] -> int    (jint (f-of r) "strict"))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------- patterns --
  ;; The reference's PATH regex, minus the surrounding backticks, which the
  ;; split has already consumed. Anchored, because the reference's backticks
  ;; anchor it: a segment must be a path in its entirety.
  (def-shell path-pat [] -> string
    "^[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\\.(md|hs|llmll|json|sh|yaml|yml|py|cabal|txt)$")

  ;; The reference's PLACEHOLDER, character for character. `\b` is supported by
  ;; TDFA and measured working; `\.` is the literal dot.
  (def-shell ph-pat [] -> string
    "NN|<|\\bfoo\\b|Mylib|cell_|turn_|\\.\\.\\.")

  ;; The reference's HIST_LINE under `re.I`, as per-letter bracket classes.
  ;; Verbose and exact. `**DONE**` needs its asterisks made literal, and a
  ;; bracketed `[*]` is used rather than a backslash escape so that nothing
  ;; depends on how the LLMLL literal and the ERE parser share a backslash.
  (def-shell hist-pat [] -> string
    (string-concat
      (string-concat
        "[*][*][Dd][Oo][Nn][Ee][*][*]|[Mm][Oo][Vv][Ee][Dd] [Tt][Oo]|[Rr][Ee][Ll][Oo][Cc][Aa][Tt]"
        "|[Ff][Oo][Rr][Mm][Ee][Rr][Ll][Yy]|[Pp][Rr][Ee][Vv][Ii][Oo][Uu][Ss][Ll][Yy]")
      (string-concat
        "|[Oo][Ll][Dd] [Pp][Aa][Tt][Hh]|[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee][Dd] [Tt][Oo]"
        "|[Rr][Ee][Mm][Oo][Vv][Ee][Dd] 20|[Dd][Ee][Ll][Ee][Tt][Ee][Dd] 20|[Mm][Ii][Gg][Rr][Aa][Tt][Ee][Dd] [Ff][Rr][Oo][Mm]")))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------- file filters --
  ;; F1: the reference drops these two prefixes from `git ls-files '*.md'`.
  ;; Measured: the six files this removes contain ZERO citations between them,
  ;; so this filter is invisible to any corpus and only cover cell 13 can see
  ;; it. It is implemented anyway, because a port reproduces its reference.
  (def-shell excluded-dir? [f: string] -> bool
    (or (starts-with? f "site/") (starts-with? f "node_modules/")))

  (def-shell starts-with? [s: string p: string] -> bool
    (and (>= (string-length s) (string-length p))
         (= (string-slice s 0 (string-length p)) p)))

  ;; F2: historical files describe the tree AS IT WAS, so their stale-looking
  ;; paths are accurate history. The reference calls this "the big one: the
  ;; difference between a ~450-item problem and a ~50-item one"; measured here
  ;; it suppresses 268 findings.
  (def-shell historical-file? [f: string] -> bool
    (or (or (= f "CHANGELOG.md")
            (string-contains f "/runs/"))
        (or (string-contains f "/postmortem-")
            (or (starts-with? f "docs/archive/")
                (string-contains f "/findings/")))))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------- the scan --
  ;; Fence stripping, reproducing FENCE.sub('', raw): split on the triple
  ;; backtick and keep the EVEN segments, concatenating with no separator,
  ;; which is what substituting the empty string does.
  (def-shell strip-fences [content: string] -> string
    (first (list-fold (string-split "```" content)
                      (pair "" true)
                      (fn [acc: (string, bool) seg: string]
                        (if (second acc)
                            (pair (string-concat (first acc) seg) false)
                            (pair (first acc) true))))))

  ;; Every inter-backtick segment that is a path citation. The `/` test is the
  ;; reference's own, applied BEFORE the citation is counted
  ;; (doc_path_lint.py:149-150), so a bare filename never reaches the total.
  (def-shell citations [body: string] -> list[string]
    (list-filter (string-split "`" body)
                 (fn [s: string] (and (string-contains s "/")
                                      (regex-match (path-pat) s)))))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------ resolution --
  ;; Membership in the tracked set, delimited so a suffix cannot match.
  (def-shell tracked? [tracked: string p: string] -> bool
    (string-contains tracked (string-concat "\n" (string-concat p "\n"))))

  ;; os.path.dirname: everything before the last separator, or "" if none.
  ;; Same fold as drop-last, and deliberately the same function rather than a
  ;; second one that could drift from it.
  (def-shell dir-of [f: string] -> string (drop-last f))

  ;; normpath(join(d, p)), folded over the segments: ".." drops the previous
  ;; component and "." is dropped. 58 of 947 live citations contain "..", so
  ;; this is on the main path rather than an edge case.
  (def-shell norm-join [d: string p: string] -> string
    (list-fold (string-split "/" (if (string-empty? d) p (string-concat d (string-concat "/" p))))
               ""
               (fn [acc: string seg: string]
                 (if (= seg "..")
                     (drop-last acc)
                     (if (or (= seg ".") (string-empty? seg))
                         acc
                         (if (string-empty? acc) seg (string-concat acc (string-concat "/" seg))))))))

  ;; Drop the last "/"-separated component. The fold carries a PENDING segment
  ;; and flushes it one step late, so the final segment is never flushed. The
  ;; obvious alternative, counting segments and rebuilding all but the last,
  ;; needs an index the fold does not supply.
  (def-shell drop-last [acc: string] -> string
    (first (list-fold (string-split "/" acc)
                      (pair "" "")
                      (fn [a: (string, string) seg: string]
                        (pair (if (string-empty? (second a))
                                  (first a)
                                  (if (string-empty? (first a))
                                      (second a)
                                      (string-concat (first a)
                                                     (string-concat "/" (second a)))))
                              seg)))))

  ;; --------------------------------------------------------- string helpers --
  ;; Everything before the first occurrence of sep, or all of s if absent.
  (def-shell before-first [s: string sep: string] -> string
    (unwrap-or (list-head (string-split sep s)) s))

  ;; Everything after the first occurrence of sep, rejoined, or "" if absent.
  ;; The counter distinguishes "before the first separator" (dropped) from the
  ;; first kept segment (no separator prepended) from the rest (separator
  ;; restored), which a bool cannot do in one pass.
  (def-shell after-first [s: string sep: string] -> string
    (first (list-fold (string-split sep s)
                      (pair "" 0)
                      (fn [a: (string, int) seg: string]
                        (if (= (second a) 0)
                            (pair "" 1)
                            (if (= (second a) 1)
                                (pair seg 2)
                                (pair (string-concat (first a) (string-concat sep seg))
                                      (+ (second a) 1))))))))

  ;; LINK.sub(']()', body): blank out every link TARGET before scanning, so a
  ;; path inside `](...)` is not read as a citation.
  ;;
  ;; MEASURED NO-OP ON THE LIVE CORPUS: 955 citations with it and 955 without,
  ;; because a target is not backticked and so is not a citation under PATH in
  ;; the first place. It fires only on a backticked target, of which this tree
  ;; has none. Implemented because a port reproduces its reference, and cover
  ;; cell 3 is its only instrument.
  (def-shell strip-links [body: string] -> string
    (first (list-fold (string-split "](" body)
                      (pair "" true)
                      (fn [a: (string, bool) seg: string]
                        (if (second a)
                            (pair seg false)
                            (pair (string-concat
                                    (first a)
                                    (if (string-contains seg ")")
                                        (string-concat "]()" (after-first seg ")"))
                                        (string-concat "](" seg)))
                                  false))))))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- ALLOW --
  ;; D5: the entries are carried HERE and not derived, because §8
  ;; deletes the reference and takes the table with it. Each one is a human
  ;; judgement that a specific citation is correct despite not resolving;
  ;; regenerating it from the tree would make every unresolved citation
  ;; self-justifying, which is the vacuous shape §7 rejects.
  ;;
  ;; Keyed on (file, path) exactly as the reference keys it, tab-separated and
  ;; newline-delimited so a membership test cannot match across two entries.
  ;;
  ;; FIVE ENTRIES ADDED AT RETIREMENT, all naming scripts/doc_path_lint.py, the
  ;; reference this port replaced. Deleting it broke 13 citations in 6 files,
  ;; measured before the deletion rather than discovered after it. Two were
  ;; present-tense and were repointed at this port instead. These five are
  ;; past-tense records of what the reference measured while it existed: a
  ;; frozen gate-figure table, two restart records, a closed work row and a
  ;; frozen harness-findings log. Rewriting them to name this port would
  ;; falsify them, because this port did not take those measurements. This is
  ;; the case the table exists for: the citation is right and the file's
  ;; absence is the point of the sentence.
  (def-shell allow-tbl [] -> string
    (string-concat
      (string-concat
        "\nexperiments/language-comparison-backlog.md\texperiments/repair-loop/BACKLOG.md\nREADME.md\tsrc/Lib.hs\nLLMLL.md\tsrc/FFI/Mylib.hs\n"
        "experiments/minimal-agent/README.md\t.llmll/templates/ecommerce-order-handler/scaffold.ast.json\nexperiments/minimal-agent/experiments/009-transfer-conservation.md\t.llmll/templates/transfer-conservation/scaffold.ast.json\n")
      (string-concat
        (string-concat
          "experiments/minimal-agent/experiments/010-byte-saturate.md\t.llmll/templates/byte-saturate/scaffold.ast.json\nexperiments/minimal-agent/experiments/011-assume-guarantee-order.md\t.llmll/templates/assume-guarantee-order/scaffold.ast.json\n"
          "experiments/r5-validation/findings.md\tscratchpad/r5drive.sh\ndocs/design/rfc-swarm-roadmap-proposal.md\taudit/runner.py\n")
        (string-concat
          (string-concat
            "docs/design/driver-in-llmll-campaign.md\tarchive/do_notation/do-notation-design.md\ndocs/design/driver-in-llmll-campaign.md\tdesign/rfc-swarm-roadmap-proposal.md\ndocs/design/driver-in-llmll-campaign.md\tdesign/driver-in-llmll-campaign.md\n"
            "docs/design/examples-audit-2026-07-20-compiler-followups.md\texamples/conways_life_json_verifier/life.ast.json.verified.json\nexperiments/repair-loop/findings.md\texperiments/repair-loop/runs/20260514T233334Z-reprobe-pbt45-c01c02c03-v0.10.6-candidate/c02/solution.k1.llmll\n")
          (string-concat
            (string-concat
              "docs/design/tool-rfc-005-doc-path-lint.md\tscripts/doc_path_lint.py\ndocs/design/tool-ll-RESTART.md\tscripts/doc_path_lint.py\ndocs/design/driver-ll-phase4-RESTART.md\tscripts/doc_path_lint.py\n"
              "docs/design/driver-ll-open-work.md\tscripts/doc_path_lint.py\nexperiments/rfc-swarm/DRIVER-LL-PHASE4-HARNESS-FINDINGS.md\tscripts/doc_path_lint.py\n")
            ;; The retirement's own record: the roadmap's shipped-release row
            ;; names the reference the release deleted. It was added by the
            ;; retirement pass and caught by rerunning this port over the edited
            ;; tree, which is the same measurement the retirement rule asks for
            ;; and the reason it is worth doing twice.
            ;;
            ;; A SECOND ENTRY HERE WAS REMOVED ON 2026-08-17 and the removal is
            ;; the point of this note. It paired the 005 RFC with
            ;; scripts/doc_path_lint_cover.py, because the retirement deleted
            ;; that cover too. The cover is REBUILT, so the citation resolves on
            ;; its own and the entry suppressed nothing. Left in place it would
            ;; have masked the next deletion of that file rather than reporting
            ;; it. AN ALLOW ENTRY OUTLIVES ITS REASON SILENTLY: nothing here
            ;; fails when an entry stops being needed, so check the table
            ;; whenever a file it names comes back.
            "docs/compiler-team-roadmap.md\tscripts/doc_path_lint.py\n"
            ;; THREE ENTRIES ADDED AT TOOL-RFC-004'S RETIREMENT, 2026-08-17, all
            ;; naming scripts/doc_archive_gate.sh. Deleting it broke SIX
            ;; citations in five files, measured before the deletion. Two were
            ;; present-tense and were repointed at the port instead: the
            ;; UPDATE-PROTOCOL row that says what the gate asserts, and the
            ;; fixture README that says what reads the fixtures. These four are
            ;; past-tense records of the reference: a shipped-release row for
            ;; v0.14.68 that says the script was NEW then, a restart record's
            ;; "The reference" table row, and the RFC's own status line, which
            ;; measures the reference at 125 code lines. Repointing any of them
            ;; at the port would falsify the sentence, because the port is not
            ;; what v0.14.68 shipped and is not 125 lines.
            ;;
            ;; THE MEASUREMENT METHOD CHANGED AND THE RULE HAD NOT NOTICED.
            ;; "Move the file aside, run the gate, restore" was written against
            ;; the Python reference, which resolved with os.path.exists. This
            ;; port resolves by `git ls-files` membership, per D2 above, so a
            ;; file moved aside is still TRACKED and still resolves. That method
            ;; silently measured nothing from the moment 005 retired. The
            ;; working method removes the file from the index as well.
            "docs/compiler-team-roadmap.md\tscripts/doc_archive_gate.sh\ndocs/design/tool-ll-RESTART.md\tscripts/doc_archive_gate.sh\ndocs/design/tool-rfc-004-doc-archive.md\tscripts/doc_archive_gate.sh\n"
            ;; NINE ENTRIES ADDED AT TOOL-RFC-003'S RETIREMENT, 2026-08-17, all
            ;; naming scripts/doc_claims_gate.sh. Deleting it broke SEVENTEEN
            ;; citations in nine files. Two were present-tense and now name the
            ;; port: the UPDATE-PROTOCOL row that says which gate executes the
            ;; compiler, and rfc-genre-and-naming's "exists because" sentence.
            ;; One was neither: the SKIP-SILENT-1 roadmap row, which is OPEN and
            ;; describes a defect the port inherited, so it was rewritten rather
            ;; than repointed.
            ;;
            ;; These seven are past-tense records. A dated triage from
            ;; 2026-07-19, a phase-01 implementation plan, the RFC's own status
            ;; line measuring the reference at 97 code lines, two citations of
            ;; the reference's own lines 1-22, the driver campaign's note of
            ;; which workflow line invoked it, and an effect-channel proposal
            ;; dating the wiring to v0.14.57, and a roadmap line that dates the same
            ;; wiring to v0.14.57 and cites the workflow line that carried it.
            ;; The ninth is THE RETIREMENT'S OWN PROSE: the fixtures README now
            ;; says the shell gate was deleted on 2026-08-17, and saying so cites
            ;; it. The 005 retirement recorded this exact effect, four citations
            ;; added by its own text, so it is expected rather than surprising.
            ;; Two of these nine the lint found only AFTER the first batch was
            ;; written, which is the loop working: edit, run the lint, repeat. Each sentence is about the
            ;; reference; naming the port would make them false.
            (string-concat
              "docs/design/critique-2026-07-19-triage.md\tscripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\ndocs/design/driver-ll-phase01-implementation-plan.md\tscripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\ndocs/design/tool-rfc-003-doc-claims.md\tscripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\ndocs/design/archive-organization-proposal.md\t../../scripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\ndocs/design/archive-organization-proposal.md\tscripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\ndocs/design/driver-in-llmll-campaign.md\tscripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\ndocs/design/effect-response-channel-proposal.md\tscripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\ndocs/compiler-team-roadmap.md\tscripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\nscripts/doc-claims/README.md\tscripts/doc_claims_gate.sh\n"
              ;; FOUR ENTRIES ADDED AT TOOL-RFC-002'S RETIREMENT, 2026-08-17, all
              ;; naming scripts/refute-crux-gate.sh. Deleting it broke EIGHT
              ;; citations in eight files, measured with the working method: remove
              ;; the file from the index, move it aside, run this port, restore.
              ;;
              ;; Four were repointed rather than allowed, and they are the reason
              ;; the split is worth stating. One is an actionable INSTRUCTION in an
              ;; examples audit, "wire this example into the gate if a bad twin is
              ;; added", which now names the port's `families` list and the
              ;; per-suite manifest. One is the RFC's own §1 subject line, kept in
              ;; the past tense with the deletion dated. One is the driver README's
              ;; account of two design changes the gate needed, both of which the
              ;; port INHERITED, so the sentence is about the port now. The fourth
              ;; was a live error the retirement did not cause: the
              ;; token-revocation README says the `stack build` preflight lives
              ;; inside the gate, and it moved to the gate's CALLERS at a23e361, so
              ;; that entry had been wrong for a release before the file went away.
              ;;
              ;; These four are past-tense records and naming the port would
              ;; falsify each one. A shipped-release row for v0.14.35, which says
              ;; the script was NEW then. A frozen gate-figure table in the closed
              ;; DRIVER-LL restart record, measured at 7fcd9d3, whose neighbouring
              ;; row already allows the retired scripts/doc_path_lint.py for the
              ;; same reason. A phase-4a plan's context list, which cites the
              ;; reference's own line 49. And an rfc-swarm findings log describing
              ;; what the reference read at its lines 86-92.
              (string-concat
                "docs/compiler-team-roadmap.md\tscripts/refute-crux-gate.sh\ndocs/design/driver-ll-phase4-RESTART.md\tscripts/refute-crux-gate.sh\ndocs/design/driver-ll-phase4a-implementation-plan.md\tscripts/refute-crux-gate.sh\nexperiments/rfc-swarm/DRIVER-LL-PORT-FINDINGS.md\tscripts/refute-crux-gate.sh\n"
                ;; THREE MORE, AND THEY ARE THE RETIREMENT'S OWN PROSE. Every one
                ;; of the three files repointed above now explains that the shell
                ;; gate was deleted, and explaining that cites it. This is the
                ;; FOURTH retirement in a row to do it: 005 added four such
                ;; citations, 004 added one, 003 added one, and this one adds
                ;; three. It is a property of the edit and not a mistake in it, so
                ;; the loop is edit, run this port, add what it names, repeat.
                "docs/design/examples-audit-2026-07-20-compiler-followups.md\tscripts/refute-crux-gate.sh\ndocs/design/tool-rfc-002-refute-crux.md\tscripts/refute-crux-gate.sh\nexamples/token-revocation-emergent/README.md\tscripts/refute-crux-gate.sh\n")))))))

  (def-shell allowed? [f: string p: string] -> bool
    (string-contains (allow-tbl)
                     (string-concat "\n" (string-concat f (string-concat "\t" (string-concat p "\n"))))))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- labels --
  ;; S3: `p` is rescued when it appears as a link LABEL whose TARGET resolves,
  ;; [`old/path.md`](new/path.md). The link works and the label is display text.
  ;;
  ;; The reference builds a per-file set of such labels, so ANY link carrying
  ;; the label with a resolving target rescues EVERY citation of it in that
  ;; file. That is why the fold below keeps looking after the first occurrence:
  ;; a file may cite the same label twice with only one link.
  (def-shell label-ok? [tracked: string f: string body: string p: string] -> bool
    (second (list-fold (string-split (string-concat "[`" (string-concat p "`](")) body)
                       (pair true false)
                       (fn [a: (bool, bool) seg: string]
                         (if (first a)
                             (pair false (second a))
                             (pair false
                                   (or (second a)
                                       (tracked? tracked
                                                 (norm-join (dir-of f)
                                                            (before-first (before-first seg ")") "#"))))))))))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------ line lookup --
  ;; The reference takes the FIRST line containing `p`, over the UNSTRIPPED
  ;; lines, and consults only that line for the historical marker. Two
  ;; consequences are reproduced rather than fixed, per §9: a path cited twice
  ;; yields two findings both carrying the first line's number, and a marker on
  ;; that first line suppresses both.
  (def-shell line-of [content: string p: string] -> int
    (second (list-fold (string-split "\n" content)
                       (pair 0 0)
                       (fn [a: (int, int) line: string]
                         (pair (+ (first a) 1)
                               (if (and (= (second a) 0)
                                        (string-contains line (string-concat "`" (string-concat p "`"))))
                                   (+ (first a) 1)
                                   (second a)))))))

  (def-shell line-text [content: string n: int] -> string
    (first (list-fold (string-split "\n" content)
                      (pair "" 1)
                      (fn [a: (string, int) line: string]
                        (pair (if (= (second a) n) line (first a))
                              (+ (second a) 1))))))

  ;; --------------------------------------------------------------- verdict --
  ;; The six suppressors of doc_path_lint.py:153-161, gathered for one citation
  ;; and handed to the PROVED adjudicator in adjudicate.llmll. The evidence is
  ;; collected here, where nothing is provable, and the decision is taken there,
  ;; where it is: `reports?` verifies body-faithful and dropping a single
  ;; suppressor from it is refuted at constraint #0.
  ;;
  ;; THE LINE LOOKUP IS DEFERRED ON PURPOSE. The reference computes it only
  ;; after S1 to S5 have all missed (`:159`, below the `or`-chain), so a
  ;; resolving citation never pays for a scan of the file's lines. Computing it
  ;; eagerly here would be correct and would walk every line of every file once
  ;; per citation, which on this corpus is 955 scans instead of the handful the
  ;; reporting path actually needs.
  ;; THE ORDER OF EVALUATION HERE IS A PERFORMANCE PROPERTY, NOT A STYLE ONE,
  ;; and it mirrors the reference's own short-circuit `or`. Two suppressors are
  ;; expensive: S3 rescans the whole file body looking for a link carrying this
  ;; label, and S6 walks every line looking for the citation. The other four are
  ;; a substring test or a regex over one short path. Evaluating the cheap four
  ;; first takes S3 from 955 body scans to the 59 citations that reach it.
  ;;
  ;; Measured: computing S3 eagerly for every citation ran past two minutes on
  ;; this corpus, because a 274 KB file with 94 citations rescans 26 MB. LLMLL is
  ;; strict, so `and`/`or` in one expression would not have saved it; the
  ;; branching is what defers the work.
  ;;
  ;; Passing `false` for a suppressor that was never evaluated is sound because
  ;; `reports?` is a negated disjunction: once any suppressor holds the answer is
  ;; false whatever the others are.
  (def-shell report-citation? [tracked: string f: string content: string
                               body: string p: string] -> bool
    (let [(root  (tracked? tracked p))
          (rel   (tracked? tracked (norm-join (dir-of f) p)))
          (ph    (regex-match (ph-pat) p))
          (allow (allowed? f p))]
      (if (or (or root rel) (or ph allow))
          (reports? root rel false ph allow false)
          (if (label-ok? tracked f body p)
              (reports? root rel true ph allow false)
              (reports? root rel false ph allow
                        (regex-match (hist-pat)
                                     (line-text content (line-of content p))))))))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------- scan a file --
  (def-shell finding-line [f: string content: string p: string] -> string
    (string-concat "  " (string-concat f (string-concat ":"
      (string-concat (int-to-string (line-of content p))
                     (string-concat "  `" (string-concat p "`\n")))))))

  ;; One file: count every citation, report the ones nothing suppressed, and
  ;; raise the reporting-file count exactly once however many findings it has.
  ;; TWO BODIES, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE OF IT. The reference builds its
  ;; label set from the FENCE-STRIPPED body and applies the link substitution
  ;; only to the copy it scans for citations (doc_path_lint.py:145-148). Passing
  ;; the link-stripped copy to the label lookup blanks every target before it is
  ;; read, so no label ever resolves.
  ;;
  ;; MEASURED, and it is why this comment exists: collapsing the two produced 20
  ;; false findings on the live tree, every one of them an S3 case, while the
  ;; other 939 citations agreed with the reference exactly. A spot check of any
  ;; single resolving citation would have passed.
  (def-shell scan-file [r: Rc f: string content: string] -> Rc
    (let [(fbody (strip-fences content))
          (before (found-of r))]
      (let [(r2 (list-fold (citations (strip-links fbody))
                           r
                           (fn [acc: Rc p: string]
                             (let [(acc2 (with-i acc "cites" (+ (cites-of acc) 1)))]
                               (if (report-citation? (tracked-of acc2) f content fbody p)
                                   (with-s (with-i acc2 "found" (tally (found-of acc2) true))
                                           "out" (string-concat (out-of acc2)
                                                                (finding-line f content p)))
                                   acc2)))))]
        (with-i (with-i r2 "scanned" (+ (scanned-of r2) 1))
                "ffiles" (if (> (found-of r2) before)
                             (+ (jint (f-of r2) "ffiles") 1)
                             (jint (f-of r2) "ffiles"))))))

  ;; ----------------------------------------------------------------- queue --
  (def-shell qhead [q: string] -> string (before-first q "\n"))
  (def-shell qrest [q: string] -> string (after-first q "\n"))
  (def-shell qempty? [q: string] -> bool (string-empty? (qhead q)))

  ;; The scannable file list: `git ls-files '*.md'` minus F1 minus F2.
  (def-shell wanted? [f: string] -> bool
    (and (not (string-empty? f))
         (and (not (excluded-dir? f)) (not (historical-file? f)))))

  (def-shell build-queue [listing: string] -> string
    (list-fold (list-filter (string-split "\n" listing) (fn [f: string] (wanted? f)))
               ""
               (fn [acc: string f: string] (string-concat acc (string-concat f "\n")))))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- report --
  ;; The reference's exact message text. The summary line is unconditional; the
  ;; two tails are mutually exclusive.
  (def-shell summary [r: Rc] -> string
    (string-concat "DRIFT-DOC-4 (advisory): "
      (string-concat (int-to-string (cites-of r))
        (string-concat " prose path citations in "
          (string-concat (int-to-string (scanned-of r)) " living files\n")))))

  (def-shell epilogue [] -> string
    (string-concat
      "\nEach is one of: a stale citation to fix, or a case ALLOW should record with a reason.\n"
      "This lint does not fail the build; see the module docstring for why it must not."))

  (def-shell report-text [r: Rc] -> string
    (if (= (found-of r) 0)
        (string-concat (summary r) "DRIFT-DOC-4: all resolve.")
        (string-concat (summary r)
          (string-concat "DRIFT-DOC-4: "
            (string-concat (int-to-string (found-of r))
              (string-concat " do not resolve, in "
                (string-concat (int-to-string (jint (f-of r) "ffiles"))
                  (string-concat " file(s).\n\n"
                    (string-concat (out-of r) (epilogue))))))))))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ Ctl --
  ;; TWO TERMINAL PHASES, AND THE SECOND ONE IS NOT REDUNDANT. `:done?` is
  ;; consulted on the state the step RETURNS, and the Command that step returns
  ;; is not performed once it holds. So a phase that both prints the report and
  ;; declares itself finished loses the report. Measured the hard way: collapsing
  ;; these two produced a run of 176 empty lines and no output at all, exiting 0.
  ;; `Ending` issues the report and is NOT done; `Done` is. docarchive.llmll
  ;; carries the same pair for the same reason.
  (type Ctl
    (| Boot)
    (| LsAll)
    (| ReadAll)
    (| LsMd)
    (| ReadMd)
    (| ReadFile)
    (| Ending)
    (| Done))

  (def-shell go [r: Rc p: Ctl c: Command] -> ((Rc, Ctl), Command)
    (pair (pair r p) c))

  (def-shell rc-of  [s: (Rc, Ctl)] -> Rc  (first s))
  (def-shell ctl-of [s: (Rc, Ctl)] -> Ctl (second s))

  (def-shell entries-of [x: Response] -> list[string]
    (match x
      ((RList xs) xs)
      ((RNone)    [])
      ((RText t)  [])
      ((RCode n)  [])
      ((RErr e)   [])))

  (def-shell text-of [x: Response] -> string
    (match x
      ((RText t)  t)
      ((RList xs) "")
      ((RNone)    "")
      ((RCode n)  "")
      ((RErr e)   "")))

  ;; Paths for the two `git ls-files` captures. wasi.proc.run writes the child's
  ;; stdout to a FILE, so each listing is a run followed by a read.
  (def-shell all-path [r: Rc] -> string (string-concat (work-of r) "/pathlint-all.txt"))
  (def-shell md-path  [r: Rc] -> string (string-concat (work-of r) "/pathlint-md.txt"))

  (def-shell run-git [r: Rc args: list[string] out: string] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run "git" args "." out out 120 "/dev/null"))

  ;; Start the file loop, or go straight to the report on an empty queue.
  (def-shell next-file [r: Rc] -> ((Rc, Ctl), Command)
    (if (qempty? (queue-of r))
        (go r Ending (wasi.io.stdout (report-text r)))
        (go (with-s r "cur" (qhead (queue-of r))) ReadFile
            (wasi.fs.read (qhead (queue-of r))))))

  ;; --------------------------------------------------------------- the step --
  (def-shell pl-step [s: (Rc, Ctl) input: string x: Response] -> ((Rc, Ctl), Command)
    (let [(r (rc-of s))]
      (match (ctl-of s)
        ;; argv arrives as r0 through RC-3. D4: `--strict` is read HERE and not
        ;; from the environment, because no env builtin exists. Measured: a
        ;; flag-shaped argument reaches a built binary intact with no `--`.
        ((Boot)
         (let [(r2 (with-s (with-i r "strict"
                             (if (list-contains (entries-of x) "--strict") 1 0))
                           "work" "."))]
           (go r2 LsAll (run-git r2 ["ls-files"] (all-path r2)))))
        ;; D2: one enumeration of the index answers every existence question.
        ((LsAll)   (go r ReadAll (wasi.fs.read (all-path r))))
        ((ReadAll)
         (let [(r2 (with-s r "tracked" (string-concat "\n" (text-of x))))]
           (go r2 LsMd (run-git r2 ["ls-files" "*.md"] (md-path r2)))))
        ((LsMd)    (go r ReadMd (wasi.fs.read (md-path r))))
        ((ReadMd)
         (next-file (with-s r "queue" (build-queue (text-of x)))))
        ;; An unreadable file is skipped rather than crashing the run. The
        ;; reference would raise; this gate reports and never fails, so raising
        ;; on one file would lose the other 171 reports.
        ((ReadFile)
         (next-file (with-s (scan-file r (cur-of r) (text-of x))
                            "queue" (qrest (queue-of r)))))
        ((Ending)  (go r Done (wasi.io.stdout "")))
        ((Done)    (go r Done (wasi.io.stdout ""))))))

  (def-shell pl-done? [s: (Rc, Ctl)] -> bool
    (match (ctl-of s) ((Done) true) ((Ending) false) ((Boot) false)
                      ((LsAll) false) ((ReadAll) false) ((LsMd) false)
                      ((ReadMd) false) ((ReadFile) false)))

  ;; THE EXIT STATUS IS THE WHOLE OF THIS GATE'S "DECISION" AND IT IS ALMOST
  ;; ALWAYS 0. `--strict` is the only way to get a 1, and only with findings.
  ;; Do not widen this without reading the module header.
  (def-shell pl-status [s: (Rc, Ctl)] -> int
    (status-of (strict-of (first s)) (found-of (first s))))

  (def-shell pl-init [] -> ((Rc, Ctl), Command)
    (pair (pair (mk json-object) Boot) wasi.proc.args))

  (def-main
    :mode    console
    :init    (pl-init)
    :step    pl-step
    :done?   pl-done?
    :status  pl-status))
